1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,500
Chapter 39: Sterilization.

3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:21,600
Puerperal Tubal Sterilization Nonpuerperal Tubal Sterilization Long-Term Complications Transcervical Sterilization Vasectomy References Among women using contraception in the United States, 28 percent rely on either male or female sterilization .

4
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:31,812
Tubal interruption or excision is suitable for those requesting sterilization and who clearly understand its permanence and its difficult and often unsuccessful reversal.

5
00:00:31,812 --> 00:00:36,050
Alternative contraceptive choices also should be presented.

6
00:00:36,050 --> 00:00:49,762
Ultimately, following provision of information, patient autonomy and her decision for sterilization should be respected (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2017, 2019).

7
00:00:49,762 --> 00:00:56,850
Female sterilization is usually accomplished by occlusion, excision, or division of the fallopian tubes.

8
00:00:56,850 --> 00:01:06,050
Puerperal sterilization procedures follow cesarean or vaginal delivery and approximately 7 percent of all live births in the United States .

9
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:13,987
Nonpuerperal tubal sterilization is done at a time unrelated to recent pregnancy and is also termed interval sterilization.

10
00:01:13,987 --> 00:01:24,025
More recently, consideration of total salpingectomy for sterilization and for ovarian cancer risk reduction is now recommended (p. 682).

11
00:01:24,025 --> 00:01:26,725
Puerperal Tubal Sterilization.

12
00:01:26,725 --> 00:01:28,750
# Timing.

13
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:38,350
Several days postpartum, the uterine fundus lies at the level of the umbilicus, and fallopian tubes are accessible directly beneath the abdominal wall.

14
00:01:38,350 --> 00:01:45,000
Moreover, abdominal laxity allows easy repositioning of the incision over each uterine cornu.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:53,512
On our service, puerperal tubal ligation is performed the morning after delivery by a surgical team designated solely to this role.

16
00:01:53,512 --> 00:02:01,650
This timing minimizes hospital stay but lowers the likelihood that postpartum hemorrhage would complicate recovery following surgery.

17
00:02:01,650 --> 00:02:06,387
The status of the newborn also can be better ascertained before surgery.

18
00:02:06,387 --> 00:02:15,550
In contrast, some prefer to perform sterilization immediately following delivery and use neuraxial analgesia already placed for labor.

19
00:02:15,550 --> 00:02:20,862
Designating these postpartum surgeries as nonelective can help lessen barriers.

20
00:02:20,862 --> 00:02:30,975
This is especially so for high-volume labor and delivery units, which typically prioritize their limited operatingroom availability for intrapartum procedures .

21
00:02:30,975 --> 00:02:39,062
From one large series, postpartum tubal ligation was a safe, reasonable option, regardless of body mass index .

22
00:02:39,062 --> 00:02:41,562
# Method Selection.

23
00:02:41,562 --> 00:02:51,175
In general for postpartum sterilization, a midtubal segment of tube is excised, and the severed ends seal by fibrosis and peritoneal regrowth.

24
00:02:51,175 --> 00:02:57,325
Commonly used methods include the Parkland, Pomeroy, and modified Pomeroy techniques.

25
00:02:57,325 --> 00:03:08,687
Less often, Filshie clips are used, and evidence points to slightly decreased efficacy (Madari, 2011; Rodriquez, 2011, 2013).

26
00:03:08,687 --> 00:03:22,025
Also, in the absence of uterine or other pelvic disease, hysterectomy solely for sterilization is difficult to justify because of its significantly higher risk for surgical morbidity compared with tubal sterilization.

27
00:03:22,025 --> 00:03:27,362
Most pelvic serous cancers are thought to originate from the distal fallopian tube .

28
00:03:27,362 --> 00:03:40,387
Because of this, although currently theoretical, evidence suggests that bilateral salpingectomy may lower these ovarian cancer rates (Falconer, 2015; Lessard-Anderson, 2014).

29
00:03:40,387 --> 00:03:53,775
With this knowledge, the Society of Gynecologic Oncologists and American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b) recommend consideration of salpingectomy to lower these cancer risks.

30
00:03:53,775 --> 00:04:06,512
Specifically, for women at average risk of ovarian cancer, risk-reducing salpingectomy should be discussed and considered with abdominopelvic surgery, with hysterectomy, or in lieu of tubal ligation.

31
00:04:06,512 --> 00:04:11,075
In the discussion of salpingectomy, several points are instructive.

32
00:04:11,075 --> 00:04:16,475
First, the lifetime risk for ovarian cancer approximates 1 percent .

33
00:04:16,475 --> 00:04:29,050
Data from epidemiologic studies show that tubal interruption alone offers an approximate 30-percent decline in ovarian cancer rates (Rice, 2012; Sieh, 2013).

34
00:04:29,050 --> 00:04:34,075
Salpingectomy may reduce the risk by 42 to 78 percent .

35
00:04:34,075 --> 00:04:44,275
However, no prospective studies of sufficient size or duration have yet been done to demonstrate the true risk and benefit ratio for women at low risk of ovarian

36
00:04:44,428 --> 00:04:45,965
cancer.

37
00:04:45,915 --> 00:04:52,140
Also, few data describe the effects on ovarian reserve from tubal blood supply disruption.

38
00:04:52,140 --> 00:05:05,940
In small studies comparing the two surgeries, no differences in antimüllerian hormone levels, which are a measure of ovarian reserve, were found (Findley, 2013; Ganer Herman, 2017).

39
00:05:05,940 --> 00:05:18,303
In other comparisons, total salpingectomy at cesarean delivery takes 5 to 10 minutes longer than partial salpingectomy (Ferrari, 2019; Powell, 2017).

40
00:05:18,303 --> 00:05:27,328
Blood loss rates are comparable or slightly higher but do not lead to higher transfusion rates or greater postsurgical declines in hematocrit values.

41
00:05:27,328 --> 00:05:37,028
Only a few small studies have evaluated salpingectomy following vaginal delivery (Danis, 2016; Powell, 2017).

42
00:05:37,028 --> 00:05:39,190
# Technique.

43
00:05:39,190 --> 00:05:45,240
Spinal analgesia is typically selected for cases scheduled on the first postpartum day.

44
00:05:45,240 --> 00:05:51,553
General anesthesia may be less desirable due to residual pregnancy-related aspiration risks .

45
00:05:51,553 --> 00:06:00,378
If done more proximate to delivery, the same epidural catheter used for labor analgesia can be used for sterilization analgesia.

46
00:06:00,378 --> 00:06:08,403
Generally, with thrombocytopenia-related conditions, platelet levels should be >70,000 for spinal blockade .

47
00:06:08,403 --> 00:06:12,815
The bladder is emptied before surgery to avoid its laceration.

48
00:06:12,815 --> 00:06:18,440
A full bladder can also push the fundus and tubes above the umbilicus and incision.

49
00:06:18,440 --> 00:06:23,440
Considered a clean case, antibiotic prophylaxis is not required.

50
00:06:23,440 --> 00:06:28,703
To begin, a small infraumbilical incision is ideal for several reasons.

51
00:06:28,703 --> 00:06:33,353
As noted, the fundus in most cases lies near the umbilicus.

52
00:06:33,353 --> 00:06:43,878
Second, the umbilicus usually remains the thinnest portion of the anterior abdominal wall and requires less subcutaneous dissection to reach the linea alba fascia.

53
00:06:43,878 --> 00:06:52,790
Third, an infraumbilical incision offers fascia with sufficient integrity to provide a closure with minimal risk for later incisional hernia.

54
00:06:52,790 --> 00:07:00,065
Last, incisions that follow the natural curve of the lower umbilical skin fold yield suitable cosmesis.

55
00:07:00,065 --> 00:07:07,015
A 2- to 3-cm transverse or vertical skin incision is usually sufficient for normal-weight women.

56
00:07:07,015 --> 00:07:13,928
For obese women, a 4- to 5-cm incision may be needed for adequate abdominal access.

57
00:07:13,928 --> 00:07:20,728
Beneath this incision, the subcutaneous tissue is bluntly separated to reach the linea alba fascia.

58
00:07:20,728 --> 00:07:26,303
For this, an Allis clamp can be opened and closed as downward pressure is exerted.

59
00:07:26,303 --> 00:07:34,740
Similarly, the blades of two armynavy retractors both pulling in downward yet opposite directions can part the subcutaneous layer.

60
00:07:34,740 --> 00:07:44,515
Clearing this fatty tissue away from the fascia isolates the fascia for incision and for later closure without intervening fat, which may impede wound healing.

61
00:07:44,515 --> 00:07:50,915
The fascial incision may be transverse or vertical and follows the same orientation of the skin incision.

62
00:07:50,915 --> 00:07:59,840
For this, once the linea alba is reached, it is grasped with two Allis clamps—one placed on either side of the planned fascial incision .

63
00:07:59,840 --> 00:08:06,553
The purchase of tissue with each clamp should be substantial and creates a small roll of fascia to be incised.

64
00:08:06,553 --> 00:08:11,565
Often, the peritoneum is incorporated simultaneously and entered.

65
00:08:11,565 --> 00:08:16,853
If not, the peritoneum is grasped with two hemostats and sharply cut.

66
00:08:16,853 --> 00:08:21,140
Others may prefer to bluntly enter with a single index finger.

67
00:08:21,140 --> 00:08:27,778
Notably, if the initial fascial incision is too small, it can be extended with curved Mayo scissors.

68
00:08:27,778 --> 00:08:30,365
Figure 39-1.

69
00:08:30,365 --> 00:08:35,865
Fascia is grasped by two Allis clamps and elevated away from viscera beneath.

70
00:08:35,865 --> 00:08:39,478
Planned fascial incision is marked by dashed line.

71
00:08:39,478 --> 00:08:46,153
Modified and reproduced with permission from Kho KA: Diagnostic and operative laparoscopy.

72
00:08:46,153 --> 00:08:55,465
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap, III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

73
00:08:55,465 --> 00:08:59,953
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

74
00:08:59,953 --> 00:09:05,828
Adequate exposure is critical, and army-navy or appendiceal retractors are suitable.

75
00:09:05,828 --> 00:09:11,753
For obese women, a slightly larger incision and narrow deeper retractors may be needed.

76
00:09:11,753 --> 00:09:17,815
If bowel or omentum is obstructing, Trendelenburg position can help displace these cephalad.

77
00:09:17,815 --> 00:09:29,465
Digitally packing with a single, moist, fanned-out piece of surgical gauze also can be used, but a hemostat should always be attached to the distal end to avert its intraabdominal retention.

78
00:09:29,465 --> 00:09:33,615
At times, mechanically tilting the entire table to the

79
00:09:33,772 --> 00:09:38,772
opposite side of the tube being exposed also can assist tube isolation.

80
00:09:38,722 --> 00:09:44,209
The fallopian tube is identified and grasped at its midportion with a Babcock clamp.

81
00:09:44,209 --> 00:09:49,447
A second slightly more distal clamp grasps the tube, which is similarly lifted.

82
00:09:49,447 --> 00:09:52,722
This allows the fimbriated end to be seen.

83
00:09:52,722 --> 00:09:57,909
Such confirmation prevents confusing the round ligament for the fallopian tube.

84
00:09:57,909 --> 00:10:04,784
A common reason for sterilization failure is ligation of the wrong structure, typically the round ligament.

85
00:10:04,784 --> 00:10:11,022
If the tube is inadvertently dropped, it is mandatory to repeat this identification process.

86
00:10:11,022 --> 00:10:17,434
Surgical steps for ligation are outlined in Figures 39-2 and 39-3.

87
00:10:17,434 --> 00:10:20,009
Figure 39-2.

88
00:10:20,009 --> 00:10:21,897
Parkland method.

89
00:10:21,897 --> 00:10:29,147
A. An avascular site in the mesosalpinx adjacent to the fallopian tube is perforated with a small hemostat.

90
00:10:29,147 --> 00:10:37,222
The jaws are opened to separate the fallopian tube from the adjacent mesosalpinx for approximately 2.5 cm. B.

91
00:10:37,222 --> 00:10:43,222
The isolated tubal portion is ligated proximally and distally with 0-chromic suture.

92
00:10:43,222 --> 00:10:51,134
The intervening segment of approximately 2 cm is excised, and the excision site is inspected for hemostasis.

93
00:10:51,134 --> 00:10:59,297
This method was designed to avoid the initial intimate proximity of the cut ends of the fallopian tube inherent with the Pomeroy procedure.

94
00:10:59,297 --> 00:11:07,659
Reproduced with permission from Hoffman BL, Hamid CA, Corton MM: Surgeries for benign gynecologic conditions.

95
00:11:07,659 --> 00:11:14,884
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al: Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

96
00:11:14,884 --> 00:11:18,809
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

97
00:11:18,809 --> 00:11:21,447
Figure 39-3.

98
00:11:21,447 --> 00:11:23,309
Pomeroy method.

99
00:11:23,309 --> 00:11:33,534
During ligation of a midsegment tubal loop, plain catgut is used to ensure prompt absorption of the ligature and subsequent separation of the severed tubal ends.

100
00:11:33,534 --> 00:11:41,897
Reproduced with permission from Hoffman BL, Hamid CA, Corton MM: Surgeries for benign gynecologic conditions.

101
00:11:41,897 --> 00:11:49,122
In Hoffman BL, Schorge JO, Halvorson LM, et al: Williams Gynecology, 4th ed.

102
00:11:49,122 --> 00:11:53,047
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2020.

103
00:11:53,047 --> 00:12:02,797
For total salpingectomy, the umbilical incision generally will need to be larger to allow an adequate view of the tube and mesosalpinx and to place clamps .

104
00:12:02,797 --> 00:12:07,584
The entire mesosalpinx must be divided to free the fallopian tube.

105
00:12:07,584 --> 00:12:19,247
Thus, risks include bleeding from the often large, congested mesosalpingeal veins, control of which requires extension of the laparotomy incision or even an adnexectomy.

106
00:12:19,247 --> 00:12:29,447
Some prefer to use a bipolar electrosurgical coagulation device (LigaSure, ENSEAL), which seals and divides the mesosalpinx simultaneously.

107
00:12:29,447 --> 00:12:32,584
This may add speed but also expense.

108
00:12:32,584 --> 00:12:35,234
Figure 39-4.

109
00:12:35,234 --> 00:12:41,447
A. With salpingectomy, the mesosalpinx is sequentially clamped, cut, and ligated.

110
00:12:41,447 --> 00:12:49,434
B. At the cornu, clamps are placed across the fallopian tube and its adjacent mesosalpinx prior to tubal transection.

111
00:12:49,434 --> 00:12:54,484
Reproduced with permission from Stuart GS: Puerperal sterilization.

112
00:12:54,484 --> 00:13:03,797
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap, III, et al [eds]: Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

113
00:13:03,797 --> 00:13:08,284
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

114
00:13:08,284 --> 00:13:13,559
After surgery, diet is given as tolerated, and walking is encouraged.

115
00:13:13,559 --> 00:13:18,897
Ileus is infrequent and should prompt concern for bowel injury, albeit rare.

116
00:13:18,897 --> 00:13:24,797
Most women have an uncomplicated course and are discharged on the first postoperative day.

117
00:13:24,797 --> 00:13:27,797
Nonpuerperal Tubal Sterilization.

118
00:13:27,797 --> 00:13:50,847
These techniques and other modifications basically consist of (1) ligation and resection at laparotomy as described earlier for puerperal sterilization; (2) application of permanent rings, clips, or inserts to the fallopian tubes by laparoscopy or hysteroscopy; or (3) electrocoagulation of a tubal segment, usually through a laparoscope.

119
00:13:50,847 --> 00:13:57,397
A detailed description and illustration of these can be found in Williams Gynecology, 4th edition .

120
00:13:57,397 --> 00:14:03,834
In the United States, a laparoscopic approach to interval tubal sterilization is the most common.

121
00:14:03,834 --> 00:14:10,147
The procedure is frequently performed in an ambulatory surgical setting under general anesthesia.

122
00:14:10,147 --> 00:14:15,322
In almost all cases, the patient can be discharged within several hours.

123
00:14:15,322 --> 00:14:23,297
Minilaparotomy using a 3-cm suprapubic incision also is popular, especially in resource-poor countries.

124
00:14:23,297 --> 00:14:28,397
Major morbidity is rare with either minilaparotomy or laparoscopy.

125
00:14:28,397 --> 00:14:34,972
Although not often used, the peritoneal cavity can be entered through the posterior vaginal fornix

126
00:14:35,140 --> 00:14:38,740
via colpotomy to perform tubal interruption.

127
00:14:38,690 --> 00:14:41,177
Long-Term Complications.

128
00:14:41,177 --> 00:14:44,002
# Contraceptive Failure.

129
00:14:44,002 --> 00:14:47,652
Pregnancy following sterilization is infrequent.

130
00:14:47,652 --> 00:14:59,502
The Collaborative Review of Sterilization (CREST) study followed 10,863 women who had undergone tubal sterilization from 1978 through 1986 .

131
00:14:59,502 --> 00:15:08,415
The cumulative failure rate for the various tubal procedures was 18.5 per 1000 or approximately 0.5 percent.

132
00:15:08,415 --> 00:15:12,915
The study found puerperal sterilization to be highly effective.

133
00:15:12,915 --> 00:15:19,727
The 5-year failure rate was 5 per 1000, and at 12 years, it was 7 per 1000.

134
00:15:19,727 --> 00:15:23,790
Puerperal sterilization fails for two major reasons.

135
00:15:23,790 --> 00:15:30,690
First, surgical errors include transection of the round ligament or only partial transection of the tube.

136
00:15:30,690 --> 00:15:36,252
For this reason, both tubal segments are submitted for pathological confirmation.

137
00:15:36,252 --> 00:15:43,190
Second, a fistulous tract or spontaneous reanastomosis may form between the severed tubal stumps.

138
00:15:43,190 --> 00:15:49,390
Approximately 30 percent of pregnancies that follow failed tubal sterilization are ectopic.

139
00:15:49,390 --> 00:15:57,565
This rate is 20 percent for those following a postpartum procedure (Peterson, 1996, 1997).

140
00:15:57,565 --> 00:16:05,652
Thus, any symptoms of pregnancy in a woman after tubal sterilization must be investigated to exclude ectopic pregnancy.

141
00:16:05,652 --> 00:16:08,052
# Other Effects.

142
00:16:08,052 --> 00:16:14,540
Women who have undergone tubal sterilization are highly unlikely to subsequently have salpingitis .

143
00:16:14,540 --> 00:16:27,727
Most studies also find that rates of heavy menstrual or intermenstrual bleeding are not increased following the procedure (DeStefano, 1985; Peterson, 2000; Shy, 1992).

144
00:16:27,727 --> 00:16:37,890
Moreover, in the CREST study, Costello (2002) found that tubal ligation did not change sexual interest or pleasure in 80 percent of women.

145
00:16:37,890 --> 00:16:45,277
In most of the 20 percent of women who did report a change, positive effects were 10 to 15 times more likely.

146
00:16:45,277 --> 00:16:57,490
Invariably, some women express regrets regarding sterilization, and this is especially true if it is performed at a younger age (Curtis, 2006; Kelekçi, 2005).

147
00:16:57,490 --> 00:17:06,940
In the CREST study, Jamieson (2002) reported that 7 percent of women who had undergone tubal ligation had regrets by 5 years.

148
00:17:06,940 --> 00:17:16,252
This is not limited to their own sterilization, because 6.1 percent of women whose husbands had undergone vasectomy had similar regrets.

149
00:17:16,252 --> 00:17:19,552
# Tubal Sterilization Reversal.

150
00:17:19,552 --> 00:17:29,102
No woman should undergo tubal sterilization believing that subsequent fertility is guaranteed either by surgery or by assisted reproductive technologies.

151
00:17:29,102 --> 00:17:34,352
Both approaches are technically difficult, expensive, and not always successful.

152
00:17:34,352 --> 00:17:48,352
In general, pregnancy rates after tubal reversal favor women with 7 cm of remaining tube, with age younger than 35 years, with a short time from antecedent sterilization, and with isthmic–isthmic repairs.

153
00:17:48,352 --> 00:17:59,415
With reanastomosis via laparotomy, rates of live births range from 44 to 82 percent (Deffieux, 2011; Malacova, 2015).

154
00:17:59,415 --> 00:18:04,690
The rate of ectopic pregnancy is 2 to 10 percent after reanastomosis .

155
00:18:04,690 --> 00:18:17,277
With reanastomosis to reverse Essure sterilization, the subsequent live birth rates range from 0 to 27 percent (Fernandez, 2014; Monteith, 2014).

156
00:18:17,277 --> 00:18:19,990
Transcervical Sterilization.

157
00:18:19,990 --> 00:18:25,527
A transcervical approach to reach the tubal ostia can be used for sterilization.

158
00:18:25,527 --> 00:18:31,040
However, no methods using this approach are currently approved in the United States.

159
00:18:31,040 --> 00:18:37,727
Mechanical methods employ insertion of a device into the proximal fallopian tubes via hysteroscopy.

160
00:18:37,727 --> 00:18:44,327
Both the Essure system and the Adiana Permanent Contraception system have been removed from the U.S. market.

161
00:18:44,327 --> 00:18:53,615
The Adiana is a cylindrical, nonabsorbent silicone elastomer matrix that once inserted stimulates tissue ingrowth to occlude the tubal lumen .

162
00:18:53,615 --> 00:19:00,090
The Essure Permanent Birth Control System is a long, slender, coiled, metallic tubal insert.

163
00:19:00,090 --> 00:19:06,365
Once placed, fibroblastic proliferation within the device similarly causes tubal occlusion.

164
00:19:06,365 --> 00:19:16,627
With this method, contraceptive failure rates range from <1 percent to 5 percent (Gariepy, 2014; Munro, 2014).

165
00:19:16,627 --> 00:19:30,227
Chronic pelvic pain after hysteroscopic sterilization may develop in 2 to 6 percent of those with Essure inserts (Chudnoff, 2015; Kamencic, 2016; Yunker, 2015).

166
00:19:30,227 --> 00:19:35,977
Pain may stem from tubal perforation, device migration, or the device itself .

167
00:19:35,977 --> 00:19:41,065
For those desiring removal, proximal linear salpingostomy and

168
00:19:41,236 --> 00:19:43,723
device removal is feasible .

169
00:19:43,673 --> 00:19:52,761
Importantly, device removal is not curative in all symptomatic patients (Clark, 2017; Maassen, 2019).

170
00:19:52,761 --> 00:20:04,173
Other cited adverse events include abnormal bleeding and allergy or hypersensitivity reaction to its components (Al-Safi, 2013; Mao, 2015).

171
00:20:04,173 --> 00:20:12,986
Chemical agents may also be placed into the uterine cavity or tubal ostia to incite an inflammatory response to cause tubal occlusion.

172
00:20:12,986 --> 00:20:22,073
Used in lowerresource areas, one method employs an intrauterine device–type inserter to place quinacrine pellets into the uterine fundus.

173
00:20:22,073 --> 00:20:28,948
From randomized trials, pregnancy rates were 1 and 12 percent at 1 and 10 years, respectively .

174
00:20:28,948 --> 00:20:36,998
The World Health Organization (2009) has recommended against quinacrine use because of carcinogenesis concerns.

175
00:20:36,998 --> 00:20:43,223
Evidence is contradictory, and some consider it a potential option for resource-poor countries .

176
00:20:43,223 --> 00:20:44,986
Vasectomy.

177
00:20:44,986 --> 00:20:54,636
Currently, up to a half million men in the United States undergo vasectomy each year (Barone, 2006; Eisenberg, 2010).

178
00:20:54,636 --> 00:20:59,386
And, 5 percent of women rely on this method for contraception .

179
00:20:59,386 --> 00:21:06,198
For sterilization, the vas deferens lumen is disrupted to block the passage of sperm from the testes .

180
00:21:06,198 --> 00:21:08,911
Figure 39-5.

181
00:21:08,911 --> 00:21:13,761
Anatomy of male reproductive system showing procedure for vasectomy.

182
00:21:13,761 --> 00:21:21,473
Vasectomy is safer than female tubal sterilization because it is less invasive and is performed with local analgesia .

183
00:21:21,473 --> 00:21:31,286
In a review that compared the two, female tubal sterilization had a 20-fold higher complication rate and a 10- to 37-fold higher failure rate .

184
00:21:31,286 --> 00:21:36,698
One disadvantage is that sterilization following vasectomy is not immediate.

185
00:21:36,698 --> 00:21:45,886
Sperm stored in the reproductive tract beyond the interrupted vas deferens takes approximately 3 months or 20 ejaculations for complete release.

186
00:21:45,886 --> 00:21:54,348
The American Urological Association recommends a postprocedural semen analysis at 8 to 16 weeks to document sterility .

187
00:21:54,348 --> 00:22:00,261
Before azoospermia is documented, another form of contraception should be used.

188
00:22:00,261 --> 00:22:10,873
The failure rate for vasectomy during the first year is 9.4 per 1000 procedures but only 11.4 per 1000 at 2, 3, and 5 years .

189
00:22:10,873 --> 00:22:23,361
Failures result from unprotected intercourse too soon after ligation, incomplete vas deferens occlusion, or recanalization (Awsare, 2005; Deneux-Tharaux, 2004).

